          %blockquote
            %p I believe that we lack another analysis properly geometric or linear which expresses location directly as algebra expresses magnitude.
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G.+W.+Leibniz"} G. W. Leibniz
              (letter to Huygens 1679, according to
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#Bredon93"} Bredon 93, p. 430
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %strong Topology
            is one of the basic fields of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+structure"} topological structure
            for that.
          %p
            The subject of topology deals with the expressions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+map"} continuity
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary"} boundary
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces"} spaces
            and relations of subspaces, which do not change under continuous deformations, regardless to other (such as in their metric properties).
          %p
            Topology as a structure enables one to model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuity"} continuity
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convergence"} convergence
            locally. More recently, in metric spaces, topologists and geometric group theorists started looking at asymptotic properties at large, which are in some sense dual to the standard topological structure and are usually referred to as
            = succeed "." do
              %em coarse topology
          %p
            There are many cousins of the concept of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sites"} sites
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locales"} locales
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topoi"} topoi
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+topoi"} higher topoi
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniformity+spaces"} uniformity spaces
            and so on, which specialize or generalize some aspect or structure usually found in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
          %p
            One of the tools of topology,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+fibrant+objects"} categories of fibrant objects
            and so on.
          %h2#Introduction Introduction
          %p The following gives a quick introduction to some of the core concepts and tools of topology:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#Continuity"} Continuity
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#TopologicalSpaces"} Topological spaces
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#Homeomorphisms"} Homeomorphism
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#Homotopy"} Homotopy
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#ConnectedComponents"} Connected components
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#FundamentalGroups"} Fundamental group
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#CoveringSpaces"} Covering spaces
          %p
            A detailed introduction is going to be at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Introduction+to+Topology"} Introduction to Topology
          %h3#Continuity Continuity
          %p
            The key idea of topology is to study
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces"} spaces
            with “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+maps"}> continuous maps
            ” between them. The concept of continuity was made precise first in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analysis"} analysis
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epsilontic+analysis"} epsilontic analysis
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+balls"} open balls
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EpsilonDeltaDefinitionOfContinuity"}
            below. Then it was realized that this has a more elegant formulation in terms of the more general concept of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+sets"} open sets
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ContinuityBetweenMetricSpacesInTermsOfOpenSets"}
            below. Adopting the latter as the definition leads to the concept of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TopologicalSpace"}
            below.
          %p
            First recall the basic concepts from
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analysis"} analysis
          #MetricSpace.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              %strong (metric space)
            %p
              A
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
              is
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                  (the “underlying set”);
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi X
                        %mo ×
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \;\colon\; X \times X \to [0,\infty)
                  (the “distance function”) from the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
                  of the set with itself to the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonnegative+number"} non-negative
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            %p
              such that for all
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mi z
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,y,z \in X
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo ⇔
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi x
                        %mo =
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(x,y) = 0 \;\;\Leftrightarrow\;\; x = y
              %li
                %p
                  (symmetry)
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi d
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi y
                        %mo ,
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(x,y) = d(y,x)
              %li
                %p
                  (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangle+inequality"}> triangle inequality
                  )
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi d
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo ,
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo +
                          %mi d
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi y
                          %mo ,
                          %mi z
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ≥
                          %mi d
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo ,
                          %mi z
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(x,y)+ d(y,z) \geq d(x,z)
          #NormedVectorSpaceBecomesMetricSpace.num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normed+vector+space"} normed vector space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V, {\vert - \vert})
              becomes a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
              according to def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MetricSpace"}
              by setting
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi x
                      %mo −
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    d(x,y) \coloneqq {\vert x-y \vert}
                    \\,.
          #EpsilonDeltaDefinitionOfContinuity.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              %strong (epsilontic definition of continuity)
            %div{:style => "float:right;margin:0 10px 10px 0;"}
              %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/EpsilonDeltaBalls.png", :width => "250"}/
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,d_X)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Y,d_Y)
              two
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+spaces"} metric spaces
              (def.
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MetricSpace"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f \;\colon\; X \longrightarrow Y
            %p
              is said to be
              %em
                continuous at a point
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
              if for every
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon \gt 0
              there exists
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta\gt 0
              such that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi δ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi ϵ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    d_X(x,y) \lt \delta \;\;\Rightarrow\;\; d_Y(f(x), f(y)) \lt \epsilon
            %p or equivalently such that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msubsup
                      %mi B
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msubsup
                      %mi B
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f(\;B_x^\circ(\delta)\;) \;\subset\; B^\circ_{f(x)}(\epsilon)
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mo ∘
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^\circ
              denotes the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+ball"} open ball
              (definition
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#OpenBalls"}
            %p
              The function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is called just
              %em continuous
              if it is continuous at every point
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
          %p
            We now reformulate this analytic concept in terms of the simple but important concept of
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+sets"} open sets
          #OpenBalls.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              %strong (open ball)
            %p
              Let
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,d)
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
              and every
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon \in \mathbb{R}_+
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+number"} positive
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real number
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi B
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mrow
                      %mo {
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mo }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    B^\circ_x(\epsilon)
                    \\;\coloneqq\;
                    \\left\{
                    y \in X \;\vert\; d(x,y) \lt \epsilon
                    \\right\}
            %p
              for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+ball"} open ball
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/radius"} radius
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
              around
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          #OpenSubsetsOfAMetricSpace.num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              %strong (neighbourhood and open set)
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,d)
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
              (def.
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MetricSpace"}
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  A
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neighbourhood"} neighbourhood
                  of a point
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
                  is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi U
                        %mo ⊂
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in U \subset X
                  which contains some
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+ball"} open ball
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi B
                          %mi x
                          %mo ∘
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_x^\circ(\epsilon)
                  around
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                  (def.
                  = succeed ")." do
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#OpenBalls"}
              %li
                %p
                  An
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subset"} open subset
                  of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                  is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo ⊂
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset X
                  such that for every
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in U
                  it also contains a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neighbourhood"} neighbourhood
                  of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          %p
            The following picture shows a point
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+balls"} open balls
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_i
            containing it, and two of its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neighbourhoods"} neighbourhoods
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
          %p
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/NeighbourhoodsAndOpenBalls.png", :width => "500"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed from
              %a{:href => "#Munkres75"} Munkres 75
          #ContinuityBetweenMetricSpacesInTermsOfOpenSets.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              %strong (rephrasing continuity in terms of open sets)
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon X \to Y
              between
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+spaces"} metric spaces
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MetricSpace"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epsilontic+analysis"} epsilontic
              sense of def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EpsilonDeltaDefinitionOfContinuity"}
              precisely if it has the property that its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pre-images"} pre-images
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              (in the sense of def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#OpenSubsetsOfAMetricSpace"}
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .num_remark{:style => "border:solid #0000cc;background: #add8e6;border-width:2px 1px;padding:0 1em;margin:0 1em;"}
            %p
              %strong principle of continuity
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
              %em Pre-Images of open subsets are open.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              First assume that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is continuous in the epsilontic sense. Then for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi O
                      %mi Y
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O_Y \subset Y
              any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subset"} open subset
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi O
                      %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in f^{-1}(O_Y)
              any point in the pre-image, we need to show that there exists a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neighbourhood"} neighbourhood
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi O
                        %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}(O_Y)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+ball"} open ball
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi B
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_x^\circ(\epsilon)
              with
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msubsup
                        %mi B
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∘
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊂
                      %msub
                        %mi O
                        %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(B_x^\circ(\epsilon)) \subset O_Y
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi O
                      %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}(O_Y)
              is open in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %p
              Conversely, assume that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}
              takes open subsets to open subsets. Then for every
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi B
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_{f(x)}^\circ(\epsilon)
              an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+ball"} open ball
              around its image, we need to produce an open ball
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi B
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_x^\circ(\delta)
              in its pre-image. But by assumption
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msubsup
                      %mi B
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}(B_{f(x)}^\circ(\epsilon))
              contains a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neighbourhood"} neighbourhood
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              which by definition means that it contains such an open ball around
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          %h3#TopologicalSpaces Topological spaces
          %p
            Therefore we should pay attention to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
            %em characterizes
            the concept:
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              %strong (closure properties of open sets in a metric space)
            %p
              The collection of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,d)
              as in def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#OpenSubsetsOfAMetricSpace"}
              has the following properties:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  The
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection"} intersection
                  of any
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+number"} finite number
                  of open subsets is again an open subset.
              %li
                %p
                  The
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/union"} union
                  of any
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                  of open subsets is again an open subset.
            %p In particular
            %ul
              %li
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty set
                is open (being the union of no subsets)
            %p and
            %ul
              %li
                the whole set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                itself is open (being the intersection of no subsets).
          %p This motivates the following generalized definition:
          #TopologicalSpace.num_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              %strong (topological spaces)
            %p
              Given a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %em topology
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a collection
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi τ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              called the
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi τ
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\tau \subset P(X)
            %p such that this is closed under forming
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  finite
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersections"} intersections
              %li
                %p
                  arbitrary
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unions"} unions
            %p
              A
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              is a set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              equipped with such a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
          %p The following shows all the topologies on the 3-element set (up to permutation of elements)
          %p
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/TopologiesOn3ElementSet.png", :width => "400"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed from
              %a{:href => "#Munkres75"} Munkres 75
          %p It is now immediate to formally implement the
          .num_remark{:style => "border:solid #0000cc;background: #add8e6;border-width:2px 1px;padding:0 1em;margin:0 1em;"}
            %p
              %strong principle of continuity
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
              %em Pre-Images of open subsets are open.
          #ContinuousMaps.num_defn
            %h6#definition_6 Definition
            %p
              %strong (continuous maps)
            %p
              A
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
              between
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi τ
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi τ
                      %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f \colon (X, \tau_X) \to (Y, \tau_Y)
            %p
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
              between the underlying sets,
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f \colon X \longrightarrow Y
            %p
              such that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pre-images"} pre-images
              under
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              of open subsets of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              are open subsets of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            The simple definition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
            and the simple
            %em principle of continuity
            gives topology its fundamental and universal flavor. The combinatorial nature of these definitions makes topology closely related to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+logic"} formal logic
            (for more on this see at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong (the category of topological spaces)
              %br/
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functions"} continuous functions
              is clearly
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associativity"} associative
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitality"} unital
            %p One says that
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
                  constitute the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+maps"} continuous maps
                  constitute the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
                  (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphisms"}> homomorphisms
                  )
            %p
              of a
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+topological+spaces"} category of topological spaces
              (“
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"}> Top
              ” for short).
            %p
              It is useful to depict collections of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
              between them by
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagrams"} diagrams
            %p
              %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/AssociativityDiagram.png", :width => "400"}/
            %blockquote
              %p
                graphics grabbed from
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#category+theory#LawvereSchanuel09"} Lawvere-Schanuel 09
            %p
          %p Our motivating example now reads:
          #MetricTopology.num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              %strong (metric topology)
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,d)
              be a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#OpenSubsetsOfAMetricSpace"}
              constitutes a
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topology
              on the set
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              in the sense of def.
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TopologicalSpace"}
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+topology"} metric topology
            %p
              Stated more concisely: the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+balls"} open balls
              in a metric space constitute a “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basis+of+a+topology"}> basis
              ” for the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+topology"} metric topology
          %p
            One point of the general definition of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"}> topological space
            ” is that it admits constructions which intuitively should exist on “continuous spaces”, but which do not in general exist, for instance, as metric spaces:
          #DiscreteTopologicalSpace.num_example
            %h6#example_3 Example
            %p
              %strong (discrete topological space)
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              a bare
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+topology"} discrete topology
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              regards
              %em every
              subset of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              as an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subset"} open subset
          #SubspaceTopology.num_example
            %h6#example_4 Example
            %p
              %strong (subspace topology)
            %div{:style => "float:right;margin:0 10px 10px 0;"}
              %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/OpenSubsetsOfSquareInsidePlane.png", :width => "200"}/
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi τ
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, \tau_X)
              be a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0 \hookrightarrow X
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
              of the underlying set. Then the corresponding
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+subspace"} topological subspace
              has
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
              as its underlying set, and its open subsets are those subsets of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
              which arise as restrictions of open subsets of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %p
              (Also called the
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+topology"} initial topology
              of the inclusion map.)
            %p
              The picture on the right shows two open subsets inside the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/square"} square
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+subspace"} topological subspace
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane"} plane
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2
            %blockquote
              %p
                graphics grabbed from
                %a{:href => "#Munkres75"} Munkres 75
          #QuotientTopologicalSpace.num_example
            %h6#example_5 Example
            %p
              %strong (quotient topological space)
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi τ
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\tau_X)
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TopologicalSpace"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mo ∼
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    R_\sim \subset X \times X
            %p
              be an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
              on its underlying set. Then the
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+topological+space"} quotient topological space
              has
            %ul
              %li
                as underlying set the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+set"} quotient set
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mo ∼
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_{\sim}
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+classes"} equivalence classes
            %p and
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  a subset
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi O
                        %mo ⊂
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mo ∼
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O \subset X_{\sim}
                  is declared to be an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subset"} open subset
                  precisely if its
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preimage"} preimage
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi π
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi O
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi^{-1}(O)
                  under the canonical
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection+map"} projection map
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi π
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mo ∼
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\pi \colon X \to X_\sim
                %p
                  is open in
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %p
              (This is also called the
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/final+topology"} final topology
              of the projection
              = succeed ".)" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
          %p
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/QuotientOfSquareIsCylinderAndTorus.png", :width => "660"}/
          %p
            The above picture shows on the left the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/square"} square
            (a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+subspace"} topological subspace
            of the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane"} plane
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+topological+space"} quotient topological space
            obtained by identifying two opposite sides (the
            = succeed ")," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cylinder"} cylinder
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torus"} torus
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed from
              %a{:href => "#Munkres75"} Munkres 75
          #ProductTopologicalSpace.num_example
            %h6#example_6 Example
            %p
              %strong (product topological space)
            %div{:style => "float:right;margin:0 10px 10px 0;"}
              %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/ProductTopology.png", :width => "300"}/
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              two
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+topological+space"} product topological space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times Y
              has
            %ul
              %li
                as underlying set the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
                of the underlying sets of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %p and
            %ul
              %li
                its
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+sets"} open sets
                are those subsets
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi O
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O \subset X \times Y
                of the Cartesian product such that for all point
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi O
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x,y) \in O
                there exists open sets
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi O
                        %mi x
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in O_x \subset X
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi O
                        %mi Y
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \in O_Y \subset Y
                such that
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi O
                          %mi x
                        %mo ×
                        %msub
                          %mi O
                          %mi y
                        %mo ⊂
                        %mi O
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O_x \times O_y \subset O
            %blockquote
              %p
                graphics grabbed from
                %a{:href => "#Munkres75"} Munkres 75
          %p
            These constructions of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+topological+spaces"} discrete topological spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+topological+spaces"} quotient topological spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+subspaces"} topological subspaces
            and of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+topological+spaces"} product topological spaces
            are simple examples of
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            and of
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
            of topological spaces. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            of topological spaces has the convenient property that
            %em all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
            (over
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+diagrams"} small diagrams
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em
                %a{:href => "Top#UniversalConstructions"} Top – Universal constructions
          %h3#Homeomorphisms Homeomorphism
          %p
            With the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"}> topological spaces
            ) and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+maps"}> continuous maps
            ) of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            of topology thus defined, we obtain the concept of “sameness” in topology.
          %p
            To make this precise, one says that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi f
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X \overset{f}{\to} Y
          %p
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            is an
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            if there exists a morphism going the other way around
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟵
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X \overset{f^{-1}}{\longleftarrow} Y
          %p
            which is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse"} inverse
            in the sense that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∘
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi Y
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi and
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi f
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  f \circ f^{-1} = id_Y \;\;\;\;\; and \;\;\;\;\; f^{-1} \circ f = id_X
                  \\,.
          #Homeomorphism.num_defn
            %h6#definition_7 Definition
            %p
              %strong (homeomorphisms)
            %p
              An
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functions"} continuous functions
              between them is called a
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphism
            %p
              Hence this is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f \;\colon\; X \longrightarrow Y
            %p
              such that there exists an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse"} inverse
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
              the other way around
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⟵
                    %mi Y
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    X \longleftarrow Y \;\colon\; f^{-1}
            %p such that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∘
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi Y
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi and
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi X
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f \circ f^{-1} = id_{Y} \;\;\;and\;\;\; f^{-1} \circ f = id_{X}
                    \\,.
          %p
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/Homeomorphism.png", :width => "560"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed from
              %a{:href => "#Munkres75"} Munkres 75
          #OpenBallsHomeomorphicToRn.num_example
            %h6#example_7 Example
            %p
              %strong (open interval homeomorphic to the real line)
            %p
              The open
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} interval
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-1,1)
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphic"} homeomorphic
              to all of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %munder
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi homeo
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (-1,1) \underset{homeo}{\simeq} \mathbb{R}^1
                    \\,.
            %p
              An
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse"} inverse
              pair of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functions"} continuous functions
              is for instance given by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi f
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi ℝ
                              %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 1
                            %mo ,
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi x
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↦
                          %mtd
                            %mfrac
                              %mi x
                              %msqrt
                                %mrow
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mo +
                                  %msup
                                    %mi x
                                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    f &amp;\colon&amp;  \mathbb{R}^1 &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; (-1,+1)
                    \\\
                    \&amp;&amp; x &amp;\mapsto&amp; \frac{x}{\sqrt{1+ x^2}}
                    }
            %p and
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi f
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 1
                            %mo ,
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi ℝ
                              %mn 1
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi x
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↦
                          %mtd
                            %mfrac
                              %mi x
                              %msqrt
                                %mrow
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mo −
                                  %msup
                                    %mi x
                                    %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    f^{-1} &amp;\colon&amp;  (-1,+1) &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; \mathbb{R}^1
                    \\\
                    \&amp;&amp; x &amp;\mapsto&amp; \frac{x}{\sqrt{1 - x^2}}
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              Generally, every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+ball"} open ball
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#OpenBalls"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphic"} homeomorphic
              to all of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
          #HomeomorphismBetweenTopologicalAndCombinatorialCircle.num_example
            %h6#example_8 Example
            %p
              %strong (interval glued at endpoints is homeomorphic to the circle)
            %p
              As topological spaces, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} interval
              with its two endpoints identified is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphic"} homeomorphic
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Homeomorphism"}
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ∼
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %munder
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi homeo
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    [0,1]_{/(0 \sim 1)} \;\; \underset{homeo}{\simeq} \;\; S^1
                    \\,.
            %p More in detail: let
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ↪
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    S^1 \hookrightarrow \mathbb{R}^2
            %p
              be the unit
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane"} plane
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo +
                    %msup
                      %mi y
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    S^1 = \{(x,y) \in \mathbb{R}^2, x^2 + y^2 = 1\}
            %p
              equipped with the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspace+topology"} subspace topology
              (example
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SubspaceTopology"}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+topology"} metric topology
              (example
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MetricTopology"}
            %p Moreover, let
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ∼
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    [0,1]_{/(0 \sim 1)}
            %p
              be the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+topological+space"} quotient topological space
              (example
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#QuotientTopologicalSpace"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} interval
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1] \subset \mathbb{R}^1
              with its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspace+topology"} subspace topology
              by applying the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
              which identifies the two endpoints (and nothing else).
            %p Consider then the function
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f \;\colon\; [0,1] \longrightarrow S^1
            %p given by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ↦
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi cos
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mi π
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi sin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mi π
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    t \mapsto (cos(2\pi t), sin(2\pi t))
                    \\,.
            %p
              This has the property that
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(0) = f(1)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+topological+space"} quotient topological space
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mn 0
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mn 0
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 1
                            %msub
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mn 0
                                %mo ∼
                                %mn 1
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mi f
                            %mo ↘
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mover
                                  %mi f
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi S
                              %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    [0,1] &amp;\overset{}{\longrightarrow}&amp; [0,1]_{/(0 \sim 1)}
                    \\\
                    \&amp; {}_{\mathllap{f}}\searrow &amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{\tilde f}}
                    \\\
                    \&amp;&amp; S^1
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              We claim that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde f
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphism
              (definition
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Homeomorphism"}
            %p
              First of all it is immediate that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde f
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
              and by definition of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+topology"} quotient topology
              (example
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#QuotientTopologicalSpace"}
            %p
              So we need to check that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde f
              has a continuous inverse function. Clearly the restriction of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              itself to the open interval
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0,1)
              has a continuous inverse. It fails to have a continuous inverse on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1)
              and on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0,1]
              and fails to have an inverse at all on [0,1], due to the fact that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(0) = f(1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ∼
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]_{/(0 \sim 1)}
              is exactly such as to fix this failure.
          %p Similarly:
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/square"} square
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]^2
            with two of its sides identified is the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cylinder"} cylinder
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torus"} torus
          %p
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/TorusAsQuotientOfSquare.png", :width => "500"}/
          %p
            If the sides are identified with opposite orientation, the result is the
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M%C3%B6bius+strip"} Möbius strip
          %p
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/MoebiusStripAsQuotientOfSquare.png", :width => "400"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed from
              %a{:href => "#Lawson03"} Lawson 03
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            Important examples of pairs of spaces that are
            %em not
            homeomorphic include the following:
          %p
          #TopologicalInvarianceOfDimension.num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+invariance+of+dimension"}> topological invariance of dimension
                )
              %br/
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi n
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi n
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_1, n_2 \in \mathbb{N}
              but
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ≠
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_1 \neq n_2
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+spaces"} Cartesian spaces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{n_1}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{n_2}
              are
              %em not
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphic"} homeomorphic
            %p
              More generally, an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+set"} open set
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{n_1}
              is never homeomorphic to an open set in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{n_2}
              if
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ≠
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_1 \neq n_2
          %p
            The proof of theorem
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TopologicalInvarianceOfDimension"}
            is surprisingly hard, given how obvious the statement seems intuitively. It requires tools from a field called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            (notably
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brouwer%27s+fixed+point+theorem"} Brouwer's fixed point theorem
          %p
            We showcase some basic tools of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            now and demonstrate the nature of their usage by proving two very simple special cases of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+invariance+of+dimension"} topological invariance of dimension
            (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TopologicalInvarianceOfDimensionFirstSimpleCase"}
            and prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#topologicalInvarianceOfDimensionSecondSimpleCase"}
            below).
          .num_example
            %h6#example_9 Example
            %p
              %strong (homeomorphism classes of surfaces)
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-sphere"} 2-sphere
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo +
                    %msup
                      %mi y
                      %mn 2
                    %mo +
                    %msup
                      %mi z
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^2 = \{(x,y,z) \in \mathbb{R}^3 \vert x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 1\}
              is
              %em not
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphic"} homeomorphic
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torus"} torus
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi T
                        %mn 2
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ×
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^2 = S^1 \times S^1
            %p
              Generally the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphism
              class of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+manifold"} closed
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientable"} orientable
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surface"} surface
              is determined by the number of “holes” it has, its
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/genus+of+a+surface"} genus
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %h3#Homotopy Homotopy
          %p
            We have seen above that for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 1
            then the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+ball"} open ball
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi B
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_0^\circ(1)
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            is
            %em not
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphic"} homeomorphic
            to, notably, the point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast = \mathbb{R}^0
            (example
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#OpenBallsHomeomorphicToRn"}
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TopologicalInvarianceOfDimension"}
            = succeed "-ball" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-ball" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            This intuition is made precise by observing that there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
            out of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+topological+space"} product topological space
            (example
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ProductTopologicalSpace"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} interval
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ×
                  %msubsup
                    %mi B
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\eta \colon [0,1] \times B_0^\circ(1)  \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}^n
          %p which is given by rescaling:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi t
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi x
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (t,x) \mapsto t \cdot x
                  \\,.
          %p
            This continuously interpolates between the open ball and the point in that for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t = 1
            then it restricts to the defining inclusion
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi B
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_0^\circ(1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t = 0
            then it restricts to the map constant on the origin.
          %div{:style => "float:right;margin:0 10px 10px 0;"}
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/ShrinkingBalls.png", :width => "200"}/
          %p
            We may summarize this situation by saying that there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functions"} continuous functions
            of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msubsup
                            %mi B
                            %mn 0
                            %mo ∘
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ×
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mn 0
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ↘
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mi x
                                %mo ↦
                                %mn 0
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mn 0
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mo ×
                          %msubsup
                            %mi B
                            %mn 0
                            %mo ∘
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi t
                              %mo ,
                              %mi x
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mo ↦
                              %mi t
                              %mo ⋅
                              %mi x
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi ℝ
                            %mi n
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ↗
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi inclusion
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msubsup
                            %mi B
                            %mn 0
                            %mo ∘
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ×
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  B_0^\circ(1) \times \{0\}
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp; \searrow^{\mathrlap{x \mapsto 0}}
                  \\\
                  [0,1] \times B_0^\circ(1)
                  \&amp;\overset{(t,x) \mapsto t \cdot x}{\longrightarrow}&amp;
                  \\mathbb{R}^n
                  \\\
                  \\uparrow &amp; \nearrow_{\mathrlap{inclusion}}
                  \\\
                  B_0^\circ(1) \times \{1\}
                  }
          %p
            Such “continuous deformations” are called
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopies"} homotopies
          #LeftHomotopy.num_defn
            %h6#definition_8 Definition
            %p
              %strong (homotopy)
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g\colon X \longrightarrow Y
              two
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functions"} continuous functions
              between
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X,Y
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+homotopy"} (left) homotopy
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi f
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi L
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\eta \colon f \,\Rightarrow_L\, g
            %p
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi I
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\eta \;\colon\; X \times I \longrightarrow Y
            %p
              out of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+topological+space"} product topological space
              (example
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ProductTopologicalSpace"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} commuting diagram
              of the form
            %div{:style => "float:right;margin:0 10px 10px 0;"}
              %img{:src => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/AHomotopy.jpg", :width => "400"}/
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                            %mo ×
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mi id
                                  %mo ,
                                  %msub
                                    %mi δ
                                    %mn 0
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo ↘
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mi f
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mn 0
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mo ×
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mi η
                          %mtd
                            %mi Y
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mi id
                                  %mo ,
                                  %msub
                                    %mi δ
                                    %mn 1
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ↗
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mi g
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                            %mo ×
                            %mi X
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    {0} \times X
                    \\\
                    {}^{\mathllap{(id,\delta_0)}}\downarrow &amp; \searrow^{\mathrlap{f}}
                    \\\
                    [0,1] \times X  &amp;\stackrel{\eta}{\longrightarrow}&amp; Y
                    \\\
                    {}^{\mathllap{(id,\delta_1)}}\uparrow &amp; \nearrow_{\mathrlap{g}}
                    \\\
                    \\{1\} \times X
                    }
                    \\,.
            %blockquote
              %p
                graphics grabbed from J. Tauber
                %a{:href => "http://jtauber.com/blog/2005/07/01/path_homotopy/"} here
          #HomotopyEquivalence.num_defn
            %h6#definition_9 Definition
            %p
              %strong (homotopy equivalence)
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \;\colon\; X \longrightarrow Y
              is called a
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalence
              if
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  there exists a continuous function the other way around,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi g
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi Y
                          %mo ⟶
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \;\colon\; Y \longrightarrow X
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+homotopies"}> left homotopies
                  , def.
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LeftHomotopy"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi η
                      %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi g
                    %msub
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi L
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\eta_1 \;\colon\; f\circ g \Rightarrow_L id_Y
            %p and
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi η
                      %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi g
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi f
                    %msub
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi L
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi X
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\eta_2 \;\colon\; g\circ f \Rightarrow_L id_X
                    \\,.
          .num_example
            %h6#example_10 Example
            %p
              %strong (open ball is contractible)
            %p
              Any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+ball"} open ball
              (or closed ball), hence (by example
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#OpenBallsHomeomorphicToRn"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalent
              to the point
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %munder
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi homotopy
                    %mo *
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbb{R}^n \underset{homotopy}{\simeq} \ast
                    \\,.
          .num_example
            %h6#example_11 Example
            %p
              The following three
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graphs"} graphs
            %p
              %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/ThreeNonHomeoButHomotopyEquivGraphs.png", :width => "400"}/
            %p
              (i.e. the evident
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+subspaces"} topological subspaces
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane"} plane
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2
              that these pictures indicate) are not
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphic"} homeomorphic
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalent
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disk"} disk
              with two points removed, by the homotopies indicated by the following pictures:
            %p
              %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/HomotopyEquivalentsToBiAnnulus.png", :width => "400"}/
            %blockquote
              %p
                graphics grabbed from
                %a{:href => "homotopy+equivalence#Hatcher"} Hatcher
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %h3#ConnectedComponents Connected components
          %p
            Using the concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            one obtains the basic tool of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+invariants"} homotopy invariants
            of topological spaces. We introduce the simplest and indicate their use.
          .num_example
            %h6#example_12 Example
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
              between two points
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo *
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,y \;\colon\; \ast \to X
            %p
              is a continuous
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path"} path
              between these points.
          #pi0.num_defn
            %h6#definition_10 Definition
            %p
              %strong (connected components)
            %p
              The set of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+classes"} homotopy classes
              of points in a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is called its set of
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  path-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+components"} connected components
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Set
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\pi_0(X) \in Set
                    \\,.
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0(X) \simeq \ast
              consists of a single element, we say that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path-connected+topological+space"} path-connected topological space
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon X \to Y
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+map"} continuous map
              between
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+classes"} homotopy classes
              of points it induces a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
              between the corresponding connected components, which we denote by:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\pi_0(f) \;\colon\; \pi_0(X) \longrightarrow \pi_0(Y)
                    \\,.
          %p
            This construction is evidently compatible with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\pi_0(g \circ f) = \pi_0(g) \circ \pi_0(f)
          %p
            and it evidently is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitality"} unital
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\pi_0(id_X) = id_{\pi_{0}(X)}
                  \\,.
          %p
            One summarizes this by saying that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0
            is a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Top
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi Set
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\pi_0 \;\colon\; Top \longrightarrow Set
                  \\,.
          %p An immediate but important consequence is this:
          #ConnectedComponentsDistinctImpliesHeomeClassesDistinct.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              If two
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
              have sets of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+components"} connected components
              that are not in
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijection
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphic"} homeomorphic
              to each other:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≠
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi X
                    %munder
                      %mo ≠
                      %mi homeo
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\pi_0(X) \neq \pi_0(Y)
                    \\;\;\;
                    \\Rightarrow
                    \\;\;\,
                    X \underset{homeo}{\neq} Y
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              Since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0
              is
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functorial
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
              to
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphisms"} homeomorphisms
              to
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijections"} bijections
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi f
                            %mo ∘
                            %mi g
                            %mo =
                            %mi id
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi and
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi g
                            %mo ∘
                            %mi f
                            %mo =
                            %mi id
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⇒
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi π
                              %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi f
                            %mo ∘
                            %mi g
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo =
                            %msub
                              %mi π
                              %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi id
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi and
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %msub
                              %mi π
                              %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi g
                            %mo ∘
                            %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo =
                            %msub
                              %mi π
                              %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi id
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⇔
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi π
                              %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ∘
                            %msub
                              %mi π
                              %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi g
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo =
                            %mi id
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi and
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %msub
                              %mi π
                              %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi g
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ∘
                            %msub
                              %mi π
                              %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo =
                            %mi id
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    \&amp; f \circ g = id \;\;and\;\; g \circ f = id
                    \\\
                    \\Rightarrow \;\;\;\;\;\;&amp;
                    \\pi_0(f \circ g) = \pi_0(id) \;\;and \;\; \pi_0(g \circ f) = \pi_0(id)
                    \\\
                    \\Leftrightarrow \;\;\;\;\;\;
                    \&amp;
                    \\pi_0(f) \circ \pi_0(g) = id \;\;and \;\; \pi_0(g) \circ \pi_0(f) = id
                    \\end{aligned}
                    \\,.
          %p This means that we may use path connected components as a first “topological invariant” that allows us to distinguish some topological spaces.
          .num_remark{:style => "border:solid #0000cc;background: #add8e6;border-width:2px 1px;padding:0 1em;margin:0 1em;"}
            %p
              %strong principle of algebraic topology
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,\,
              %em Use topological invariants to distinguish topological spaces.
          %p
            As an example for how this is being used, we have the following proof of a simple special case of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+invariance+of+dimension"} topological invariance of dimension
            (theorem
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TopologicalInvarianceOfDimension"}
          #TopologicalInvarianceOfDimensionFirstSimpleCase.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_4 Proposition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+invariance+of+dimension"} topological invariance of dimension
                – first simple case)
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+spaces"} Cartesian spaces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2
              are not
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphic"} homeomorphic
              (def.
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Homeomorphism"}
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              Assume there were a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f \colon \mathbb{R}^1 \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}^2
            %p
              we will derive a contradiction. If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is a homeomorphism, then clearly so is its restriction to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+subspaces"} topological subspaces
              (example
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SubspaceTopology"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \in \mathbb{R}^1
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(0) \in \mathbb{R}^2
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f
                    \\;\colon\;
                    (\mathbb{R}^1-\{0\})
                    \\longrightarrow
                    (\mathbb{R}^2 - \{f(0)\})
                    \\,.
            %p
              It follows that we would get a bijection of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+components"} connected components
              between
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0(\mathbb{R}^1 - \{0\})
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0(\mathbb{R}^2  - \{f(0)\})
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≠
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\pi_0(\mathbb{R}^1-\{0\})
                    \\;\neq\;
                    \\pi_0(\mathbb{R}^2 - \{f(0)\})
                    \\,.
          %p
            The key lesson of the proof of prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TopologicalInvarianceOfDimensionFirstSimpleCase"}
            is its strategy:
          .num_remark{:style => "border:solid #0000cc;background: #add8e6;border-width:2px 1px;padding:0 1em;margin:0 1em;"}
            %p
              %strong principle of algebraic topology
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,\,
              %em Use topological invariants to distinguish topological spaces.
          %p
            Of course in practice one uses more sophisticated invariants than just
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0
          %p
            The next topological invariant after the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+components"} connected components
            is the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"}> fundamental group
              \:
          %h3#FundamentalGroups Fundamental group
          #FundamentalGroup.num_defn
            %h6#definition_11 Definition
            %p
              %strong (fundamental group)
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
              be a chosen point. Then write
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ∈
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi Grp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\pi_1(X,x)
                    \\;\in\;
                    Grp
            %p
              for, to start with, the set of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+classes"} homotopy classes
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/paths"} paths
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              that start and end at
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loops"} loops
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              based at
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  Under concatenation of loops,
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(X,x)
                  becomes a
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-group"} semi-group
              %li
                %p
                  The constant loop is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutral+element"} neutral element
                  under this composition (thus making
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(X,x)
                  a “
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"}> monoid
                  ”).
              %li
                %p
                  The reverse of a loop is its
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse"} inverse
                  in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo ,
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(X,x)
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(X,x)
                  indeed into a
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            %p
              This is called the
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              at
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          %p
            The following picture indicates the four non-equivalent non-trivial generators of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
            of the oriented
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surface"} surface
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/genus+of+a+surface"} genus
            2:
          %p
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/FundamentalGroupOfGenus2Surface.png", :width => "500"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed from
              %a{:href => "#Lawson03"} Lawson 03
          %p
            Again, this operation is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functorial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Top
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top^{\ast/}
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+topological+spaces"} pointed topological spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            are topological spaces equipped with a chosen point, and whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+maps"} continuous maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon X \to Y
            that take the chosen basepoint of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to that of
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi Top
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi Grp
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\pi_1 \;\colon\; Top^{\ast/} \longrightarrow Grp
                  \\,.
          %p
            As
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1
            is a topological invariant. As before, we may use this to prove a simple case of the theorem of the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+invariance+of+dimension"} topological invariance of dimension
          #SimplyConnected.num_defn
            %h6#definition_12 Definition
            %p
              A topological space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              for which
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ≃
                        %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0(X) \simeq \ast
                  (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path-connected+topological+space"}> path connected
                  , def.
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#pi0"}
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ≃
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(X,x) \simeq 1
                  (the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
                  is
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+group"} trivial
                  = succeed ")," do
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FundamentalGroup"}
            %p
              is called
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simply+connected+topological+space"} simply connected
          #topologicalInvarianceOfDimensionSecondSimpleCase.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_5 Proposition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+invariance+of+dimension"} topological invariance of dimension
                – second simple case)
            %p
              There is
              %em no
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphism
              between
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^3
          .proof
            %h6#proof_4 Proof
            %p
              Assume there were such a homeomorphism
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is a homeomorphism, then so is its restriction to removing the origin from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(0)
              from
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^3
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 3
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\mathbb{R}^2 - \{0\})
                    \\longrightarrow
                    (\mathbb{R}^3 - \{f(0)\})
                    \\,.
            %p
              Thse two spaces are both path-connected, hence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0
              does not distiguish them.
            %p
              But they do have different
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groups"} fundamental groups
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  The fundamental group of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mn 2
                        %mo −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{2} - \{0\}
                  is
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℤ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
                  (counting the winding of loops around the removed point). We discuss this further below in example
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FundamentalGroupOfTheCircle"}
              %li
                %p
                  The fundamental group of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mn 3
                        %mo −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^3 - \{f(0)\}
                  is trivial: because the single removed point is no obstruction to sliding loops past it and contracting them.
            %p
              But since passing to fundamental groups is
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functorial
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ConnectedComponentsDistinctImpliesHeomeClassesDistinct"}
              shows that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              cannot be an
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphism
          %p We now discuss a “dual incarnation” of fundamental groups, which often helps to compute them.
          %h3#CoveringSpaces Covering spaces
          #CoveringSpace.num_defn
            %h6#definition_13 Definition
            %p
              %strong (covering space)
            %p
              A
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering+space"} covering space
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+map"} continuous map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    p \colon E \to X
            %p
              such that there exists an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+cover"} open cover
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mo ⊔
                        %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi i
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{i}{\sqcup}U_i \to X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
              then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to X
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphic"} homeomorphic
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+topological+space"} product topological space
              (example
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ProductTopologicalSpace"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
              with the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+topological+space"} discrete topological space
              (example
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DiscreteTopologicalSpace"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_i
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ⊔
                              %mi i
                            %msub
                              %mi U
                              %mi i
                            %mo ×
                            %msub
                              %mi F
                              %mi i
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mi E
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi pb
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mi p
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ⊔
                              %mi i
                            %msub
                              %mi U
                              %mi i
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\underset{i}{\sqcup} U_i \times F_i &amp;\longrightarrow&amp;  E
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;(pb)&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{p}}
                    \\\
                    \\underset{i}{\sqcup} U_i &amp;\underset{}{\longrightarrow}&amp; X
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in U_i \subset X
              a point, then the elements in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_x  = F_i
              are called the
              %em leaves
              of the covering at
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          #kForlCovringOfCircle.num_example
            %h6#example_13 Example
            %p
              %strong (covering of circle by circle)
            %div{:style => "float:left;margin:0 10px 10px 0;"}
              %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/pFoldCoveringOfCircleB.png", :width => "180"}/
            %p
              Regard the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi z
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1 = \{ z \in \mathbb{C}  \;\vert\; {\vert z\vert} = 1 \}
              as the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+subspace"} topological subspace
              of elements of unit
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolute+value"} absolute value
              in the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+plane"} complex plane
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in \mathbb{N}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi k
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    p \coloneqq (-)^k \;\colon\; S^1 \longrightarrow S^1
            %p
              given by taking a complex number to its
              = succeed "th" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              times around itself”. Precisely, for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \geq 1
              this is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering+space"} covering space
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CoveringSpace"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              leaves at each point.
            %blockquote
              %p
                graphics grabbed from
                %a{:href => "homotopy+equivalence#Hatcher"} Hatcher
          #CoveringOfCircleByRealLine.num_example
            %h6#example_14 Example
            %p
              %strong (covering of circle by real line)
            %div{:style => "float:right;margin:0 10px 10px 0;"}
              %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/UniversalCoveringOfCircle.png", :width => "150"}/
            %p
              Consider the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi exp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mi π
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\exp(2 \pi i(-)) \;\colon\; \mathbb{R}^1 \longrightarrow S^1
            %p
              from the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
              to the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  with the circle regarded as the unit circle in the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+plane"} complex plane
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ℂ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi t
                        %mo ↦
                        %mi exp
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 2
                        %mi π
                        %mi i
                        %mi t
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        t \mapsto \exp(2\pi i t)
              %li
                %p
                  with the circle regarded as the unit circle in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi ℝ
                            %mn 2
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi t
                        %mo ↦
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi cos
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 2
                        %mi π
                        %mi t
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ,
                        %mi sin
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 2
                        %mi π
                        %mi t
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo .
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        t \mapsto ( cos(2\pi t), sin(2\pi t) )
                        \\,.
            %p
              We may think of this as the result of “winding the line around the circle ad infinitum”. Precisely, this is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering+space"} covering space
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CoveringSpace"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/leaves"} leaves
              at each point forming the set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
          #ActionOfFundamentalGroupOnFibersOfCovering.num_defn
            %h6#definition_14 Definition
            %p
              %strong (action of fundamental group on fibers of covering)
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mi π
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \overset{\pi}{\longrightarrow} X
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering+space"} covering space
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CoveringSpace"}
            %p
              Then for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
              any point, and any choice of element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e \in F_x
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/leaf+space"} leaf space
              over
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              of an element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma
              of the the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(X,x)
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FundamentalGroup"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              that starts at
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ρ
                      %mi γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi e
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho_\gamma(e) \in F_x
              in the same
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi ρ
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi F
                              %mi x
                            %mo ×
                            %msub
                              %mi π
                              %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi X
                            %mo ,
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi F
                              %mi x
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
